WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.095
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.395 --> 00:00:08.920
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:16.052
Unit five: Technological Determinism. Today you will argue about hypothetical causes with inverted conditionals.

4
00:00:16.352 --> 00:00:18.947
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.319
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:24.183 --> 00:00:26.148
Technological determinism.

7
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:31.936
Technological determinism means the view that technology drives social change.

8
00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:36.488
Technological determinism claims the printing press caused the Reformation.

9
00:00:37.083 --> 00:00:40.235
Your turn. Say: Technological determinism.

10
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.065
Technological determinism.

11
00:00:45.817 --> 00:00:47.329
Social construction.

12
00:00:48.317 --> 00:00:52.870
Social construction means the idea that society shapes how technology develops.

13
00:00:53.483 --> 00:00:57.515
Social construction explains why bicycles took different forms.

14
00:00:58.117 --> 00:01:01.338
Your turn. Say: Social construction.

15
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.863
Social construction.

16
00:01:06.617 --> 00:01:08.256
Innovation diffusion.

17
00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:13.374
Innovation diffusion means the spread of new technology through a society.

18
00:01:13.983 --> 00:01:16.935
Innovation diffusion was rapid for mobile phones.

19
00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:20.794
Your turn. Say: Innovation diffusion.

20
00:01:23.633 --> 00:01:25.099
Innovation diffusion.

21
00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:27.687
Unintended consequence.

22
00:01:28.683 --> 00:01:32.424
Unintended consequence means an unplanned result of an action.

23
00:01:33.017 --> 00:01:36.665
Social media had unintended consequences for politics.

24
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.820
Your turn. Say: Unintended consequence.

25
00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:45.759
Unintended consequence.

26
00:01:46.517 --> 00:01:48.180
Technological adoption.

27
00:01:49.183 --> 00:01:53.168
Technological adoption means the decision to start using a technology.

28
00:01:53.783 --> 00:01:57.072
Price drives technological adoption in rural areas.

29
00:01:57.683 --> 00:02:01.578
Your turn. Say: Technological adoption.

30
00:02:04.433 --> 00:02:06.444
Technological adoption.

31
00:02:07.183 --> 00:02:08.544
Digital disruption.

32
00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:14.382
Digital disruption means the transformation of industries by digital technology.

33
00:02:14.983 --> 00:02:17.842
Digital disruption closed many video shops.

34
00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.242
Your turn. Say: Digital disruption.

35
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:25.380
Digital disruption.

36
00:02:26.117 --> 00:02:27.443
Path dependency.

37
00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:32.330
Path dependency means when past choices limit future options.

38
00:02:32.917 --> 00:02:36.124
The QWERTY keyboard shows path dependency.

39
00:02:36.717 --> 00:02:39.578
Your turn. Say: Path dependency.

40
00:02:42.433 --> 00:02:44.003
Path dependency.

41
00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:46.564
Sociotechnical system.

42
00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:52.916
Sociotechnical system means a system combining people, rules and technology.

43
00:02:53.517 --> 00:02:56.213
A hospital is a sociotechnical system.

44
00:02:56.817 --> 00:03:00.409
Your turn. Say: Sociotechnical system.

45
00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:04.999
Sociotechnical system.

46
00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:07.157
Adoption curve.

47
00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:12.344
Adoption curve means a graph of how quickly groups adopt innovations.

48
00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:15.530
Teenagers sit early on the adoption curve.

49
00:03:16.117 --> 00:03:19.222
Your turn. Say: Adoption curve.

50
00:03:22.067 --> 00:03:23.474
Adoption curve.

51
00:03:24.217 --> 00:03:25.206
Feedback loop.

52
00:03:26.217 --> 00:03:30.062
Feedback loop means a process where outputs influence future inputs.

53
00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:33.462
Recommendation algorithms create a feedback loop.

54
00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:36.702
Your turn. Say: Feedback loop.

55
00:03:39.567 --> 00:03:40.765
Feedback loop.

56
00:03:41.567 --> 00:03:45.380
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:46.217 --> 00:03:52.431
Visal, did smartphones change Cambodian society, or did Cambodians change how smartphones are used?

58
00:03:53.517 --> 00:04:03.518
Both. Were technology truly deterministic, every country would use phones identically, yet here mobile payments spread faster than in many richer countries.

59
00:04:04.617 --> 00:04:06.883
So society shapes technology.

60
00:04:07.983 --> 00:04:17.465
Partly. Had banks been widely available earlier, mobile money might never have become so popular. Local conditions guided adoption.

61
00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:21.606
But technology still has effects nobody chose.

62
00:04:22.717 --> 00:04:33.751
Certainly. Should algorithms keep creating feedback loops, the unintended consequences for politics could be serious, whatever designers intended.

63
00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:36.458
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:37.317 --> 00:04:40.547
Grammar focus: Abstract conditional reasoning.

65
00:04:40.847 --> 00:04:42.022
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:42.717 --> 00:04:46.969
Were technology truly deterministic, human choice would matter less.

67
00:04:47.883 --> 00:04:52.333
Had banks been available earlier, mobile money might not have spread so fast.

68
00:04:53.217 --> 00:04:57.817
Should algorithms keep creating feedback loops, the consequences could be serious.

69
00:04:58.767 --> 00:05:10.080
Formal inversion replaces 'if': Were X (= if X were), Had X done (= if X had done), Should X (= if X happens to). Do not use 'if' as well, and keep the result clause unchanged.

70
00:05:10.817 --> 00:05:14.723
Be careful! Don't say: If were technology deterministic, …

71
00:05:16.667 --> 00:05:19.212
Say: Were technology deterministic, …

72
00:05:19.512 --> 00:05:21.024
Don't use 'if' with inversion.

73
00:05:21.833 --> 00:05:24.602
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:26.680
Who Is in Control?.

75
00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:32.520
Technological determinism holds that new technologies drive social change in predictable ways.

76
00:05:33.533 --> 00:05:39.225
Were this entirely true, societies adopting the same inventions would develop in the same direction.

77
00:05:40.233 --> 00:05:46.784
Historians of technology point out, however, that adoption is shaped by culture, economics and politics.

78
00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:54.513
At the same time, technologies create path dependencies and unintended consequences that no one deliberately chose.

79
00:05:55.500 --> 00:06:01.923
Most scholars therefore prefer the idea of sociotechnical systems, in which people and machines shape each other.

80
00:06:03.033 --> 00:06:07.081
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:06:07.917 --> 00:06:10.439
blank we known the risks, we would have acted.

82
00:06:10.739 --> 00:06:13.963
A, Had. B, If had. C, Were.

83
00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:18.896
The answer is A: Had.

84
00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:22.723
blank the price fall, adoption would increase.

85
00:06:23.023 --> 00:06:25.957
A, Should. B, Would. C, Had.

86
00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.325
The answer is A: Should.

87
00:06:32.483 --> 00:06:35.621
When past choices limit future options, this is blank.

88
00:06:35.921 --> 00:06:41.420
A, path dependency. B, digital disruption. C, social construction.

89
00:06:44.967 --> 00:06:47.338
The answer is A: path dependency.

90
00:06:48.483 --> 00:06:50.587
Choose the correct formal sentence.

91
00:06:50.887 --> 00:06:58.302
A, Were it cheaper, more people would buy it.. B, Was it cheaper, more people buy it.. C, If were it cheaper, more people would buy it..

92
00:07:01.867 --> 00:07:05.190
The answer is A: Were it cheaper, more people would buy it..

93
00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:10.196
According to the reading, most scholars prefer the idea of…

94
00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:16.738
A, pure determinism. B, sociotechnical systems. C, unintended consequences.

95
00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:23.484
The answer is B: sociotechnical systems.

96
00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:27.526
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:28.383 --> 00:07:32.705
Does technology shape society, or does society shape technology?

98
00:07:49.517 --> 00:07:50.994
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:51.294 --> 00:08:28.784
I believe technology and society shape each other, so neither side fully determines the other. Were technology the only cause, every country would use the internet in the same way, which is clearly not the case. In Cambodia, for example, mobile banking grew quickly because many people had no bank account. Nevertheless, technologies also produce unintended consequences, such as the spread of misinformation. So we should not see ourselves as passive victims of technology, but we must take its effects seriously.

100
00:08:29.084 --> 00:08:30.782
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:31.683 --> 00:08:36.216
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and abstract conditional reasoning.

102
00:08:36.516 --> 00:08:41.351
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

103
00:08:41.651 --> 00:08:44.327
See you in Unit six. Goodbye!

104
00:08:44.627 --> 00:08:47.349
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

